\caption(The native trace supplies all five pass states and the diagram's actual first-pass result. The final gap-one pass has no swaps and still performs six comparisons.)
\alttext(Seven indexed boxes show the input above its actual gap-five output. An arrow carries value two from original position five to position zero, exchanging it with five.)

The gaps are 5,3,2,1,1, so comparisons total 2+4+5+6+6=23. The first gap-one
pass finishes the ordering; the second certifies it. Records come from the
same executing kernel as comb_sort(), rather than a report-side replay.

> Correctness, inversions and what the bound says

Every exchange preserves the input multiset. The gap strictly decreases while
it exceeds one, so a logarithmic number of wide scans leads to gap one. From
then on this is ordinary full-width forward bubble sorting: the largest
remaining misplaced value reaches its final rightmost position on each successful
scan. At most n−1 successful adjacent scans and one zero-swap scan suffice.
That last scan tests every adjacent pair and establishes nondecreasing order.

A wide scan alone does not completely sort every gap-separated subsequence;
it makes one bubbling traversal. This distinction matters when comparing comb
sort with Shell sort's gapped insertion passes.

Wide scans take O(n log n) key tests in total; the final adjacent phase has
the upper bound n(n−1). Hence an O(n²) worst-case upper bound follows for this
implementation. The random experiment below does not establish a sharper
universal rate. On sorted input there are no swaps, but every wide gap is still
visited before one gap-one scan stops. The geometric schedule has Θ(log n)
scans, most costing a constant fraction of n, so sorted-input work is Θ(n log n).
Sorted bubble input needs just n−1 comparisons. A benefit on one input family
is not a guarantee of adaptivity to all nearly ordered inputs.

> The original seed-four shuffle experiment

Keep one local mt19937 engine seeded with four. For n=10,20,...,400, construct
[0,...,n−1] and consume the next std::shuffle permutation from that same engine.
This retains the original workload sequence. A seed repeats for this standard
library; the shuffle mapping is not promised identical across implementations.

Both comparison counts now come from datamunge. The original bubble helper
scanned all n−1 adjacent pairs every time, with no shrinking right boundary,
and included the final zero-swap scan. bubble_sort_work() deliberately preserves
that baseline. Its comparisons equal (n−1) times its actual scan count. The
ratio below compares those two exact kernels, rather than every optimized bubble
variant or elapsed wall-clock time. No sorting loop remains in the report.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/comb_sort.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::mt19937 engine(4);
  std::vector<double> sizes,comb_counts,bubble_counts;
  std::print("| n | actual comb comparisons | actual full-width bubble comparisons | comb gap-one scans | bubble scans |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t n=10;n<=400;n+=10) {
    std::vector<int> input(n);std::iota(input.begin(),input.end(),0);
    std::shuffle(input.begin(),input.end(),engine);
    const auto comb=dm::algorithms::comb_sort_work(input),bubble=dm::algorithms::bubble_sort_work(input);
    if(comb.output!=bubble.output || !std::is_sorted(comb.output.begin(),comb.output.end()) || bubble.key_comparisons!=(n-1)*bubble.pass_count)return 1;
    sizes.push_back(n);comb_counts.push_back(comb.key_comparisons);bubble_counts.push_back(bubble.key_comparisons);
    if(n==10 || n==50 || n==100 || n==200 || n==400)
      std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",n,comb.key_comparisons,bubble.key_comparisons,comb.gap_one_passes,bubble.pass_count);
  }
  std::print("\nAt n=400, measured full-width-bubble/comb comparison ratio: {:.6f}.\n",bubble_counts.back()/comb_counts.back());
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.line(sizes,bubble_counts,"full-width bubble",{220,38,38},2)
      .line(sizes,comb_counts,"comb",{37,99,235},2)
      .legend({"full-width bubble","comb"},{{220,38,38},{37,99,235}})
      .title("Original seed-four shuffles: actual key comparisons")
      .x_label("input length n").y_label("strict-greater key tests")
      .x_limits(0,420).y_limits(0,180000).size(900,580)
      .save_svg("build/comb_sort.svg");
}
```
