\caption(The original forty-size experiment uses native counters and an explicitly full-width bubble baseline. Curve labels identify measured methods without attaching an unproved random-case asymptotic rate.)
\alttext(A red full-width bubble comparison curve rises far above a blue comb comparison curve over the original forty shuffled sizes from ten to four hundred.)

This build gives 7,106 comb and 155,610 full-width bubble comparisons at n=400.
The old report's frozen bubble total was 147,630. A compatibility test runs its
original counting loops on all forty currently generated inputs and agrees with
the native counters. The executable result above follows that verified code;
the seed and generator procedure are preserved, without a cross-library promise
for std::shuffle's permutation mapping. One shuffled input per size also does
not estimate a distributional average.

> Sorted and turtle controls

Generated families isolate claims that a random curve cannot settle. Sorted
input verifies comb's compulsory gap schedule and bubble's single scan. A
turtle [1,2,...,n−1,0] makes zero travel left only once per full-width bubble
scan: n−1 swapping scans and one stopping scan give exactly n(n−1) comparisons.
Comb can carry it left by wide exchanges, but its remaining gap-one scan count
is measured rather than assumed to be one.

The table uses both families. The plot divides sorted-input comparisons by n
and places log2(n) on the horizontal axis, making comb's compulsory logarithmic
scan growth visible beside bubble's count just below one per item. This plotting
transformation changes the axes, not the native counters. No external dataset
is useful for these exact-order controls.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/comb_sorted_control.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<double> log_sizes,comb_per_item,bubble_per_item;
  std::print("| n | sorted comb comparisons | sorted bubble comparisons | turtle comb comparisons | turtle bubble comparisons | turtle comb gap-one scans |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(const std::size_t n:{8,16,32,64,128,256,512}) {
    std::vector<int> input(n);std::iota(input.begin(),input.end(),0);
    const auto sorted=dm::algorithms::comb_sort_work(input),sorted_bubble=dm::algorithms::bubble_sort_work(input);
    std::iota(input.begin(),input.end()-1,1);input.back()=0;
    const auto turtle=dm::algorithms::comb_sort_work(input),turtle_bubble=dm::algorithms::bubble_sort_work(input);
    if(sorted.swaps!=0 || sorted.gap_one_passes!=1 || sorted_bubble.key_comparisons!=n-1 || turtle_bubble.key_comparisons!=n*(n-1))return 1;
    log_sizes.push_back(std::log2(double(n)));
    comb_per_item.push_back(double(sorted.key_comparisons)/n);bubble_per_item.push_back(double(sorted_bubble.key_comparisons)/n);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",n,sorted.key_comparisons,sorted_bubble.key_comparisons,turtle.key_comparisons,turtle_bubble.key_comparisons,turtle.gap_one_passes);
  }
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.line(log_sizes,comb_per_item,"comb",{37,99,235},2).points(log_sizes,comb_per_item,"",{37,99,235},5)
      .line(log_sizes,bubble_per_item,"full-width bubble",{220,38,38},2)
      .legend({"comb","full-width bubble"},{{37,99,235},{220,38,38}})
      .title("Already sorted inputs still traverse the comb gaps")
      .x_label("log2(input length n)").y_label("actual key comparisons / n")
      .y_limits(0,25).size(900,580).save_svg("build/comb_sorted_control.svg");
}
```
