\alttext(On sorted inputs of length eight through five hundred twelve, comb's comparisons per item rise with log-two length, while full-width bubble's count per item stays below one.)

> Strict comparisons still allow unstable equal-key identities

Label the two equal values in [2a,2b,1] by their original positions zero and one.
The first gap is two. Exchanging 2a with 1 gives [1,2b,2a]; the following
gap-one scan makes no swap, so the equal twos finish reversed. They never swapped
with each other. Nonadjacent exchanges let one pass the other indirectly.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/comb_instability.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::comb_sort_trace({2,2,1});
  if(t.original_indices!=std::vector<std::size_t>{2,1,0} || t.work.swaps!=1)return 1;
  std::print("| final position | value | actual original position |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(90,950);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=1.8;box.height=1.1;box.font_size=18;
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<t.input.size();++i) {
    box.fill=t.input[i]==2?dm::graphics::RGB{219,234,254}:dm::graphics::RGB{220,252,231};
    d.node(std::format("in{}",i),{3.*i,1.5},std::format("{}\nsource {}",t.input[i],i),box);
    box.fill=t.work.output[i]==2?dm::graphics::RGB{219,234,254}:dm::graphics::RGB{220,252,231};
    d.node(std::format("out{}",i),{3.*i,-1.5},std::format("{}\nsource {}",t.work.output[i],t.original_indices[i]),box);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n",i,t.work.output[i],t.original_indices[i]);
  }
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<t.input.size();++i)
    d.edge(std::format("in{}",t.original_indices[i]),std::format("out{}",i));
  d.text({3,3},"One gapped swap reverses the two equal-key identities",22)
   .text({3,-3},"Twos start with sources [0,1] and finish [1,0]",21)
   .save_svg("build/comb_instability.svg");
  std::print("\nActual exchanges {}; initial strict inversions 2.\n",t.work.swaps);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| final position | value | actual original position |
| ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 1 | 2 |
| 1 | 2 | 1 |
| 2 | 2 | 0 |

Actual exchanges 1; initial strict inversions 2.
\image(figures/comb_instability.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Original positions follow the actual native exchanges. One wide swap removes both initial inversions, while reversing the two equal-key identities.)
\alttext(Three input boxes with values two, two and one connect to sorted output boxes. The equal twos finish with original identities one then zero, proving instability.)

More generally, exchange x>y at positions i<j. Their mutual inversion disappears.
An intervening z strictly between y and x removes two more inversions; z equal
to either endpoint removes one; other z values contribute no change. Thus

\[
I_{\rm before}-I_{\rm after}
=1+2\#\{k:i<k<j,\ y<a_k<x\}
+\#\{k:i<k<j,\ a_k=x\text{ or }a_k=y\}.
\]
\alttext(The inversion decrease is one, plus twice the number of intervening strictly-between keys, plus the number of intervening keys equal to either exchanged endpoint.)

Every gapped strict swap removes at least one inversion, so total swaps are
at most the input's strict inversion count. Equality is guaranteed for adjacent
swaps, as in the bubble baseline, but not for comb: this example needs one
exchange for two inversions. Swap count and comparison count measure different work.

> Exact gap arithmetic and API costs

The former expression (gap×10)/13 can overflow before dividing, even when the
result fits. Write gap=13q+r, with 0≤r<13. Then

\[
\left\lfloor\frac{10g}{13}\right\rfloor
=10q+\left\lfloor\frac{10r}{13}\right\rfloor.
\]
\alttext(Dividing the gap into quotient q and remainder r modulo thirteen gives an exact next gap as ten q plus the floor of ten r divided by thirteen.)

The first product and final sum are at most the original gap; the remainder
product is at most 120. This is exact size_t arithmetic, with no floating-point
shrink factor or larger integer type in the library. The helper also accepts
zero and returns one, though empty/singleton sorts skip their pass loops.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto maximum=std::numeric_limits<std::size_t>::max();
  std::print("| supplied gap | exact native next gap |\n| ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(const auto gap:std::vector<std::size_t>{0,1,7,12,13,maximum}) {
    const auto next=dm::algorithms::comb_sort_next_gap(gap);
    if(next<1 || (gap>1 && next>=gap))return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {} |\n",gap,next);
  }
  const std::vector<int> input{5,3,8,1,9,2,7};
  const auto complete=dm::algorithms::comb_sort_trace(input,5);
  bool limited=false,invalid=false;
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::comb_sort_trace(input,4);}catch(const std::length_error&){limited=true;}
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::comb_sort_trace({},0);}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){invalid=true;}
  if(!limited || !invalid || input.front()!=5 || complete.passes.size()!=5)return 1;
  std::print("\nFive records succeed; four throw length_error. Zero cap throws invalid_argument, even for empty input. Caller input is unchanged.\n");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| supplied gap | exact native next gap |
| ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 1 |
| 1 | 1 |
| 7 | 5 |
| 12 | 9 |
| 13 | 10 |
| 18446744073709551615 | 14189803133622732011 |

Five records succeed; four throw length_error. Zero cap throws invalid_argument, even for empty input. Caller input is unchanged.
// result_end
\caption(The exact helper supports SIZE_MAX without allocating a vector of that size. The trace cap counts the final zero-swap scan; work-only and legacy calls have no record cap.)

The legacy and work-only calls sort an owned input buffer with constant extra
state; an lvalue call copies the caller's vector. “In-place” describes the
internal exchanges, not caller mutation. Work counters exclude vector copies,
gap calculations, loop conditions and elapsed time, and throw on overflow.
The optional trace owns input, final identities and two length-n snapshots per
pass. With p passes it adds O(np) time/storage. Its positive default cap of
1024 records is checked online, before each pass, and throws without returning
a partial trace. All calls use independent state. Signed extreme integers are
valid because comparisons and exchanges never subtract key values.

> Conclusion

The original example takes gaps 5,3,2,1,1, twenty-three key comparisons and six
exchanges. Wide gaps help the original shuffled and turtle inputs, while sorted
inputs expose compulsory work that a single bubble scan avoids. Actual counters
support these comparisons without turning one sample curve into a universal
linearithmic claim. Carried identities show why strict gapped exchanges remain
unstable, and exact quotient/remainder arithmetic makes the gap schedule safe
throughout size_t's range.

> References

- W. Dobosiewicz (1980), [An efficient variation of bubble sort](https://doi.org/10.1016/0020-0190(80)90022-8), Information Processing Letters 11(1), 5–6. Historical diminishing-gap exchange variant; the exact schedule in this report is stated explicitly above.
- P. E. Black (2022), [Comb sort](https://xlinux.nist.gov/dads/HTML/combSort.html), NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures. Distinguishes one bubbling traversal per gap from fully sorting each gapped subsequence.
- P. E. Black and B. Bockholt, [Bubble sort](https://xlinux.nist.gov/dads/HTML/bubblesort.html), NIST Dictionary of Algorithms and Data Structures. The comparison baseline here deliberately scans the full width, as in the original report.
